          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/condensed+matter+physics"} condensed matter physics
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phases+of+matter"} topological phases of matter
            %em topological semi-metal
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystal"} crystalline
            material whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electronic+band+structure"} electronic band structure
            is such that there is a sizeable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy+gap"} energy gap
            between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valence+band"} valence band
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conduction+band"} conduction band
            (as for an
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/insulator"} insulator
            %em except
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+number"} positive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submanifold"} submanifold
            of “nodal loci” inside the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brillouin+torus"} Brillouin torus
          %p
            This terminology, which was established in the early 2000s, is meant to follow the older terminology
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiconductor"} semiconductor
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/insulator"} insulator
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metal"} metal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/insulator"} insulator
            for the bulk of its excitation modes, like a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiconductor"} semiconductor
            for excitations close to the nodal loci, and almost like a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metal"} metal
            for excitations right at the nodal loci; whence the terminology.
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/SemiMetalBandStructure-20220405.jpg", :width => "400"}/
            (graphics from
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Topological+Quantum+Computation+in+TED-K"} SS 22
          %p
            If the gap closure happens over an isolated point, one speaks of a
            %em nodal point
            proper, or more specifically of a
            %em Dirac point
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %em Weyl point
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eigenstates"} eigenstates
            at the point (2 for Weyl, 4 for Dirac, see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#MassTerms"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curve"} curve
            or a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            = succeed "." do
              %em nodal line
          %p
            Due to the gap on (a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neighbourhood+retract"} neighbourhood retract
            of) the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
            of the nodal points/lines inside the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brillouin+torus"} Brillouin torus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+connection"} Berry connection
            is well-defined away from the nodal loci, and it tends to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+connection"} flat
            in suitable hyperplanes there. It’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
            around
            = succeed "=2" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphene"} graphene
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/defect+brane"} defect brane
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+phase"} Berry phase
            – is then a property of the nodal locus alone, and to be interpreted as a measure for the “topological protection” of the gap over the nodal loci (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#FWDF16"} FWDF 16, II.B
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#Vanderbilt18"} Vanderbilt 18, 5.5.2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+adiabatic+theorem"} adiabatic
            deformations of the material that do not excite modes above the gap, these nodal points may move and merge or split, but their total topological charge cannot change.
          %p
            Therefore semi-metals with such non-trivial Berry phases are examples of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phases+of+matter"} topological phases of matter
            = succeed "," do
              %em topological semi-metals
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/insulator"} insulator
            and may then be a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+insulator"} topological insulator
            (if the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+equivariant+K-theory"} twisted equivariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            class of its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valence+bundle"} valence bundle
            is non-trivial).
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#MassTerms Phase decay via mass terms
          %p
            Near nodal points in the Brillouin torus of a semi-metal, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dispersion+relation"} dispersion relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  k \mapsto E(
                  k)
            exhibited by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy+bands"} energy bands
            is thought to be approximated by that of a massless
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+equation"} Dirac equation
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weyl+spinor"} Weyl equation
            %em Dirac point
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %em Weyl point
          %p
            When the material’s parameters can be and are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+adiabatic+theorem"} adiabatically
            tuned such that this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dispersion+relation"} dispersion relation
            turns into a
            %em massive
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+equation"} Dirac equation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficient"} coefficient
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            of the effective
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+term"} mass term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %msub
                    %mi γ
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \gamma_0
            in the Dirac equation. If this happens to all nodal points (while keeping the band gap open everywhere else), one expects that the topological semi-metal phase decays into a
            = succeed "-phase." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+insulator"} topological insulator
          %p
            A necessary condition for such a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+term"} mass term
            to exist at all is that a further
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clifford+algebra"} Clifford generator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi γ
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma_0
            is represented on the Bloch-Hilbert space of electrons such that it skew-commutes with all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+slash+notation"} Clifford momenta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k\!\!\!/
            (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Schnyder18"} Schnyder 18, Sec. II.A
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Schnyder20"} Schnyder 20, Sec. 2.1
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#FreedHopkins21"} Freed &amp; Hopkins 21, around Lem. 9.55
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/MassTermsForSemiMetalNodalPoints-220517.jpg", :width => "600px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (graphics from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#SS22"} SS22
          %p
            By
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Karoubi+K-theory"} Karoubi/Atiyah-Singer-type theorems
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#ChiuSchnyder14"} Chiu &amp; Schnyder 14, Sec. A.2
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Schnyder18"} Schnyder 18, Sec. II.A
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Schnyder20"} Schnyder 20, Sec. 2.1
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#MorimotoFurusaki13"} Morimoto &amp; Furusaki 13, Sec. V
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#CTSR15"} CTSR 15, Sec. III.C
          %p Beware:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                This means that the topological
                = succeed "-phase" do
                  %em semi-metal
                %em modulo
                the group of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+terms"} mass terms
                (cf.
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#FreedHopkins21"} Freed &amp; Hopkins 21, Thm. 9.63
                = succeed "-phase" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+insulator"} topological insulator
            %li
              %p
                The argument about single
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+terms"} mass terms
                in a K-theory group of a point ignores the global topology of the valence bundle.
              %p
                This is manifest already in the canonical example of the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haldane+model"} Haldane model
                %em constant
                mass terms (as usually understood) the total topological charge of all nodal points combined is constrained to vanish.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            The archetypical and original example of a semi-metal is
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphene"} graphene
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+points"} Dirac points
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin-orbit+coupling"} spin-orbit coupling
            in graphene actually produces a tiny gap even at the would-be Dirac points, which however tends to be too small to be visible in practice. But if one will or would analyze graphene with an accuracy that does resolve this tiny gap-opening, then graphene will or would appear as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+insulator"} topological insulator
            instead of a semi-metal.)
          %p After the synthesis of graphene, which is an effectively 2-dimensional material (an atomic mono-layer) much attention has shifted to the synthesis and understanding of 3-dimensional semi-metals.
